  "worked_solution": "Let $D$ be the event that the picked electronic tube is defective, and let $A$ be the event that the electronic tube is produced by machine ${M}_{1}.$ Then, ${A}^{\u2032}$ is the event that the electronic tube is produced by machine ${M}_{2}.$\n\nTo calculate the probability that a randomly picked tube from a day's production is defective, we can use the law of total probability:\n\n$$\nP(D)=P(D|A)P(A)+P(D|{A}^{\u2032})P({A}^{\u2032})\n$$\n\nLet's go through the problem statement and translate each bit of information into mathematical notation.\n\n- Since machine ${M}_{1}$ produces $75%$ of the total daily output of electronic tubes and ${M}_{2}$ the remaining $25%,$ we have\n\n$$\nP(A)=0.75,\\,P({A}^{\u2032})=0.25.\n$$\n\n- Since $4%$ of the tubes produced by machine ${M}_{1}$ are defective, and $5%$ of the tubes produced by machine ${M}_{2}$ are defective, we have\n\n$$\nP(D|A)=0.04,\\,P(D|{A}^{\u2032})=0.05.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above information into the law of total probability, the probability that a randomly picked tube from a day's production is defective is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(D) & =P(D|A)P(A)+P(D|{A}^{\u2032})P({A}^{\u2032}) \\\\  & =(0.04)(0.75)+(0.05)(0.25) \\\\  & =0.0425.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
